{"content": "{
  "book_value_offer": 189100,
  "independent_indication_18pct": 1764000,
  "independent_indication_6_1pct": 597800,
  "dilution_valid": false,
  "why": "Three independent grounds, each shown with arithmetic. (1) Notice mechanics: OA §6.3 requires written notice delivered to each Member by CERTIFIED MAIL AND EMAIL at least 30 days before the due date; the 2026-08-20 call was sent by TEXT MESSAGE + PDF, no certified mail was used (undisputed in the Manager's own production), and the email prong failed on a stale address. Under Zimmerman v. Crothall, as summarized in the library, dilution provisions are enforced as written but the issuer must comply strictly with the specified delivery mechanics; a call that fails the specified delivery method does not trigger dilution. No valid call -> no Dilution Event -> §9.2's book-value buyout trigger never fired. (2) The dilution arithmetic does not reconcile even internally: at the Manager's own asserted $7.2M fair value, standard pro-rata dilution of a member who funded none of a $1.5M raise yields 0.18 x 7.2/8.7 = 14.90% (new money priced at pre-money value) or 0.18 x (7.2-1.5)/7.2 = 14.25% (if $7.2M is post-money) — not 6.1%. Back-solving the notice's 6.1% for Bram (82% -> 93.9%) requires valuing the post-money company at P where 0.18 x (P-1.5M)/P = 6.1%, i.e. P = 1.5M/(1-0.061/0.18) = $2,268,877 post-money (implied pre-money $768,877). The notice's own arithmetic therefore values the company at 3.2x LESS than the $7.2M the Manager himself asserted. (3) The Manager's $7.2M 'fair value' is 27% below the contemporaneous independent broker opinion ($14.5M enterprise value; $9.8M equity). Valuation gap at a glance: offer $189,100 (6.1% x $3.1M book) is 31.6% of $597,800 (6.1% x $9.8M broker equity) and 10.7% of $1,764,000 (18% x $9.8M). Remarks: book value ($3.1M members' equity per year-end balance sheet) is 3.16x below broker equity ($9.8M) — the gap is presumably appreciated real estate not carried on the balance sheet (no appraisal in the file; confirm with client whether any company debt explains part of the gap). All percentages cross-checked: 0.061 x $3.1M = $189,100 exactly (the offer amount matches the diluted percentage only); 0.18 x $1.5M = $270,000 (Imani's unfunded share) and Bram's $1,230,000 + $270,000 = $1,500,000 ( Bram subscribed 100% of the raise, consistent with him ending at 100% - 6.1% = 93.9%)."
}
